nt in the calculator above to get your number."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How much interest will a $5,000 balance cost in a month?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"At 24% APR, a $5,000 balance is charged $98.63 in one billing cycle. That assumes a 30-day cycle, a 365-day year and no payment during the cycle. On a 360-day basis, the same cycle costs $100.00. A different APR changes the figure, so enter your own rate above."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the daily periodic rate?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The daily periodic rate is your APR divided by the days in the year. At 24% APR on a 365-day year, it is 0.0658% a day. On a 360-day year, the same APR gives 0.0667% a day."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the average daily balance?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The average daily balance is the average of each day's balance across the billing cycle. A payment lowers it from the day the payment posts. Start with $5,000 and post a $1,000 payment on day 11. Across a 30-day cycle, the balance averages $4,333.33."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does paying early in the billing cycle lower my interest?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. A payment lowers the balance for every remaining day in the cycle, so an earlier payment means a lower average daily balance. On a $5,000 balance at 24% APR, a $1,000 payment posting on day 11 drops the cycle's interest to $85.48. That is $13.15 less than the $98.63 charged with no payment."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why is the effective annual rate higher than my APR?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"When interest compounds daily, each day's interest is added to the balance and then charged interest itself. At 24% APR, daily compounding over a 365-day year gives an effective annual rate of 27.11%. Simple interest on a flat $5,000 balance comes to $1,200 for the year."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why does my statement show a slightly different amount?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Some issuers divide the APR by 365 days and some by 360. On the $5,000 example, that difference is $98.63 versus $100.00 for one cycle. Issuers also differ in how they compound within a cycle and how they round, which can shift the statement amount slightly. Set the day-count input to the basis on your statement to get closer."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does this calculator include a grace period or fees?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. It covers purchase interest only, with no grace period, fees, cash advances or new purchases. To pay a balance down, use the credit card payoff calculator."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is there a 10% credit card interest cap?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No, a 10% credit card interest cap is not federal law. Proposals S. 381 and H.R. 1944 in the 119th Congress would cap consumer credit card rates at 10% until January 2031, per a Congressional Research Service (CRS) report updated March 10, 2026. S. 381 was introduced on February 4, 2025, and referred to Senate Banking, though current status after that is not confirmed here."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How much would a 10% APR cap save on a $5,000 balance?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A 10% cap saves $57.53 in interest over one 30-day billing cycle on a $5,000 balance, dropping the charge from $98.63 at 24% to $41.10. Our calculation assumes a 365-day year, simple interest, no payments, no new fees, and the card staying available at 10%. Auxiliary fees or lost access to credit if banks limit products could erase those savings."}}]}</script><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Person","name":"Jonathan Velez","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/about/","jobTitle":"Personal Finance Writer & Editor","description":"Jonathan Velez writes and edits ModernWallet's personal-finance content, focused on loans, interest, and debt payoff. Every calculator page is reviewed against primary sources like the CFPB and Federal Reserve.","knowsAbout":["Auto loans","Loan amortization","Interest and APR","Debt payoff strategies","Personal finance"],"worksFor":{"@type":"Organization","name":"ModernWallet","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com"}}</script> <script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Article","headline":"Credit Card Interest Calculator","description":"Free credit card interest calculator. See one billing cycle's interest from your APR and average daily balance, and what a mid-cycle payment saves you.","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/credit-card-interest-calculator/","image":{"@type":"ImageObject","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/favicon.svg"},"datePublished":"2026-10-14","dateModified":"2026-11-23","author":{"@type":"Person","name":"Jonathan Velez","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/about/"},"publisher":{"@type":"Organization","name":"ModernWallet","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com","logo":{"@type":"ImageObject","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/favicon.svg"}},"citation":[{"@type":"CreativeWork","name":"Congressional Research Service — IF12861, Credit Card Interest Rate Caps","url":"https://crsreports.congress.gov/product/pdf/IF/IF12861"},{"@type":"CreativeWork","name":"govinfo — S.381, 10 Percent Credit Card Interest Rate Cap Act (as introduced)","url":"https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/BILLS-119s381is/html/BILLS-119s381is.htm"}]}</script> <main class="page" data-astro-cid-5nhdsx2r> <nav class="crumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb" data-astro-cid-5nhdsx2r> <a href="/" data-astro-cid-5nhdsx2r>ModernWallet</a> <span data-astro-cid-5nhdsx2r>/</span> <span class="crumb-current" data-astro-cid-5nhdsx2r>Credit Card Interest</span> </nav> <header class="hero" data-astro-cid-5nhdsx2r> <h1 data-astro-cid-5nhdsx2r>Credit Card Interest Calculator</h1> <div class="byline" data-astro-cid-oxbng3yr> <span class="by" data-astro-cid-oxbng3yr>By <a href="/about/" data-astro-cid-oxbng3yr>Jonathan Velez</a>, Personal Finance Writer &amp; Editor</span> <span class="sep" aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-oxbng3yr>·</span> <span class="rev" data-astro-cid-oxbng3yr>Reviewed against primary sources · Updated November 23, 2026</span> </div>   <p class="lede" data-astro-cid-5nhdsx2r>A credit card interest calculator finds one billing cycle's interest by dividing your annual percentage rate (APR) by the days in the year, then multiplying that daily rate by your average daily balance and by the number of days in the cycle. Enter your starting balance, APR, cycle length and 365- or 360-day basis in the calculator above. You can also add a payment made during the cycle and the day it posts. The calculator then shows the daily periodic rate, the average daily balance, the yearly cost if the balance stays flat, and the effective annual rate with daily compounding.</p><p class="lede" data-astro-cid-5nhdsx2r>For example, take a $5,000 balance at 24% APR with no payment during the cycle. The cycle runs 30 days, and the daily rate uses a 365-day year. That gives a daily rate of 0.0658%, an average daily balance of $5,000, and interest of $98.63 for the cycle. If a $1,000 payment posts on day 11, interest for the same cycle drops to $85.48. That payment saves $13.15 compared with paying nothing during the cycle.</p> </header> <div class="tool-wrap" data-astro-cid-5nhdsx2r> <button type="button" class="share-btn" data-share-title="Credit Card Interest Calculator" aria-label="Share this tool" data-astro-cid-qan2qojn> <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-qan2qojn><path d="M12 3v12M7.5 7.5 12 3l4.5 4.5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" data-astro-cid-qan2qojn></path><path d="M5 12v7a1 1 0 0 0 1 1h12a1 1 0 0 0 1-1v-7" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" data-astro-cid-qan2qojn></path></svg> <s